# Italian letter-to-sound.
#
# One block a letter and its arms in order, the first that matches winning.
# The last arm of a block is the bare letter: what it says when nothing else
# applies. An arm may also say what must stand to its left and right without
# swallowing it -- `after', `before', and `vowel', `consonant' or `glide' in
# either place -- and `at start' or `at end' for a run that has to begin or
# end where the piece of the word does.
#
# Italian doubles its consonants and means it: a doubled letter is two phones
# and one long sound, which IBM says by marking both `geminate'. Without the
# mark the phones are right and the sound is not, so the word gate passes and
# the sentences move. r is the exception, its double being one trill.
#
# An n before a hard c, g or q is the velar nasal, marked to be written as n,
# where IBM's rule builds that record at run time. It is the same record
# either way, and the ear cannot tell which built it.
#
# Some of what Italian says is decided by word rather than by letter, and IBM
# keeps those words in lists named for what they decide: an s between vowels
# is unvoiced in asepsi and dinosauro because their roots begin with an entry
# of VsV_pronounced_s. An arm names such a list last of all, `where the root
# begins <list>' or `where the root is <list>'.
#
# All twenty-six are here. The vowels are the most of it: whether a stressed
# e or o is open is a tree of letter tests and lists, and a later rule closes
# any open one that is not stressed, so the arms need not know which is.

rules in it_phone.obj named apply_ital_%s_rules
a piece runs from 864 to 872
a word runs from 704 to 712

